Ballast-Gouge
Ingredients: FREE JAZZ / AVANT-GARDE + THRASH METAL + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Alto Saxophone + 1960s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: The genre apes the call-and-response of container ships greeting each other in fog.

Ballast-Gouge sounds like a fleet of two-hundred-ton freighters trading territorial bellowing across a blinded bay while an amphetamine-fueled rhythm section tries to outrun a nautical disaster. It weaponizes the frantic, downpicked velocity of thrash metal, welds it to the unfettered harmonic anarchy of 1960s free jazz, and bathes the collision in cold, salt-drenched psychedelic vertigo.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 190–240 BPM. Relentless thrash skank-beats and double-bass drum barrages collide with out-of-meter jazz polyrhythms, suddenly dropping into dead-stop suspended pauses that simulate the breathless acoustic drift between horn blasts over open water.
* Harmonic Palette: Microtonal multiphonics, diminished cluster chords, and Dorian-sharp-eleven modes shoved through high-gain germanium fuzz; chords are treated not as progressions, but as massive tectonic obstacles.
* Production Signatures: 
  1. Acoustic Vapor-Chamber Lathe-Cutting (an impossible process where master acetates are cut inside an industrial brine-fog tank, forcing microscopic salt-water droplets to pit the vinyl grooves and create oceanic surface hiss).
  2. Spring-reverbed 1960s tape delays modulated by low-frequency oscillation to create blinding Doppler swoops.
  3. Spatial call-and-response panning, hard-throwing audio from the extreme left channel to the extreme right to mimic ships hailing one another through sea-smog.
* Dynamics: A typical piece swings violently between blistering, suffocating 32nd-note battery and vast, cavernous silences punctuated only by roaring, overblown alto sax notes echoing as if three miles out to sea.

Scene & Origin Lore
Ballast-Gouge ignited during the bone-chilling winter of 1968 along the soot-crusted loading gantries of San Pedro, California. Longshoremen, jazz renegades ejected from Sunset Strip clubs, and desert-dwelling acid casualties gathered after midnight on the rusted roofs of cold-storage warehouses facing the harbor channel. Wearing oilskin deck jackets, welder’s goggles smeared with petroleum jelly to induce visual distortion, and surplus military boots, the crowd passed around astronomical binoculars to track both the constellations and the blind cargo freighters creeping through the marine layer. Ritual performances were strictly unannounced: bands hauled customized tube amps and modified alto saxophones onto roof ledges, waiting for an inbound ship to blow its low horn before detonating into frantic, roaring blast-sections that matched the vessel’s pitch, effectively using oceanic traffic as an unwitting lead conductor.

Typical Instruments: 
* Overdriven Alto Saxophone (fitted with an upturned metal funnel and fed through a 1960s germanium fuzz box)
* Modified 1960s Fender Stratocasters and Gibson Rippers played with downpicked thrash speed through twin Marshall Super Lead stacks
* Double-kick 1960s Ludwig drum kit equipped with cracked marching-band cymbals
* Binson Echorec magnetic drum delay units
* Acetate tape loops of naval navigation warnings and shortwave radio static

For Fans Of: Peter Brötzmann, Power Trip, John Coltrane (Om era), Voivod

